# Every settled node under a composed layer, with its remainder carried up. One row per (composed
# layer, settled node) pair, the factors along the path already multiplied in.
#
# The recursion taken as a join
#   The stopping rule is the whole content. A fusion's remainder is defined by recursion on `F`,
#   and the natural form needs a sum in the recursive term, which SQL forbids. The recursion
#   flattens instead: substitute it into itself and the intermediate layers cancel, leaving one sum
#   with the factors multiplied along each path. A running product is a scalar per row and is
#   allowed in a recursive term, so composition/descent.sqlc's walk carries it and this is a join.
#
# The path product needs there to be one path
#   "The factors multiplied along each path" is a single number per node only where each node is
#   reached one way. Which it is, is a measurement and not a property of multiplication: it is the
#   layer graph's cycle space being zero, which rank/cycle_space.sqlc reports and
#   checks/jagged_layer is the rule for. Two paths to one node give two products and the arithmetic
#   chooses neither, which is why the guard below declines rather than picking one.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `path_product_needs_one_path`.
#
# It stops at the first settled node, not at a leaf
#   The difference is not an optimisation. A settled node's `n` and `d` were never scaled by one
#   factor with width, so differencing them is legitimate and its filed totals are a claim its
#   composer stands behind. Descend past one and those totals are bypassed, along with every
#   correction that composer already applied, which then has to be rebuilt from below and can fail
#   for reasons the filed figure had already settled. `merge-group-composition/compute-pt` is that
#   case: it files a demand, its own conversion is a point, and one of its eliminations is unsized.
#   Stopping there reads a figure; running past it reaches a correction nobody sized.
#
# A leaf is the special case
#   composition/unsettled.sqlc is the predicate, and a leaf satisfies it trivially by having no
#   parts at all. The leaf is the special case of the base case, not the base case.
#
# The same corner rule, with the path product as the driver
#   For a strictly positive product the bounds are the two corners on each side, which collapses to
#   the component-wise form when the node's remainder is non-negative and reverses when it is not.
#
# `NOT w.is_cycle` is a guard, not a detector
#   It keeps a looped path out of an arithmetic and reports nothing about one. A document whose
#   parts loop has no evaluation order at all, and `checks/layers_move_together` is what says so;
#   this relation simply declines to return a number for it.
-- composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc less the nodes composition/unsettled.sqlc names,
-- against layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc at the node the walk stops on.
SELECT w.root_filing AS composition, w.root_layer AS composed_layer,
       w.filing AS node_filing, w.layer AS node_layer, w.depth,
       w.factor_low, w.factor_mode, w.factor_high,
       least(   r.r_low  * w.factor_low, r.r_low  * w.factor_high) AS r_low,
       r.r_mode * w.factor_mode                                    AS r_mode,
       greatest(r.r_high * w.factor_low, r.r_high * w.factor_high) AS r_high,
       (w.factor_low IS DISTINCT FROM w.factor_high)               AS spread_factor
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc)
) w
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
) r ON r.filing = w.filing AND r.layer = w.layer
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/unsettled.sqlc)
) o ON o.filing = w.filing AND o.layer = w.layer
WHERE w.usable
  AND o.filing IS NULL
